prompt:
Build the MongoDB parts of the schema migration platform in package `mongodb` at backend/internal/platform/mongodb: the ledger, the schema lock, and the validator and index helpers, with integration tests. Use the driver go.mongodb.org/mongo-driver/v2 (bson package: go.mongodb.org/mongo-driver/v2/bson). Leave mongodb.go (Connect, Ping) as it is. Don't import any bounded context (internal/archtest enforces this).

Terms:
- Ledger: the `schema_migrations` collection, which records each applied migration.
- Schema lock: a lease held by the one instance that is applying migrations. It expires automatically if its holder dies (lease of at most 60 s, renewed while migrating).
- Validator: a collection's `$jsonSchema`, enforced with validationLevel strict and validationAction error.

Contract. S01-01 writes migration.go in this package in parallel with you, and a later task (S01-03) writes migrate.go against exactly these names:
```go
// ledger.go
const LedgerCollection = "schema_migrations"
type ledgerEntry struct {
    ID          string    `bson:"_id"`         // migration ID, e.g. "catalog/0001"
    Description string    `bson:"description"`
    AppliedAt   time.Time `bson:"appliedAt"`   // UTC
    DurationMs  int64     `bson:"durationMs"`
}
func appliedMigrationIDs(ctx context.Context, db *mongo.Database) ([]string, error) // all ledger IDs, sorted ascending
func recordMigration(ctx context.Context, db *mongo.Database, e ledgerEntry) error   // InsertOne; a duplicate _id is an error naming the ID

// lock.go
const LockCollection = "schema_lock" // holds one document {_id: "schema", owner: string, expiresAt: date}
var ErrSchemaLockLost = errors.New("schema lock lost")
type schemaLock struct { /* unexported fields */ }
func newSchemaLock(db *mongo.Database, owner string, lease time.Duration) *schemaLock
func (l *schemaLock) tryAcquire(ctx context.Context) (bool, error)          // one attempt; true if we hold it now
func (l *schemaLock) acquire(ctx context.Context, poll time.Duration) error // tryAcquire every poll until held, or until ctx is done
func (l *schemaLock) renew(ctx context.Context) error                       // expiresAt = now+lease; ErrSchemaLockLost if we no longer own it
func (l *schemaLock) release(ctx context.Context) error                     // delete the document only if we own it; idempotent

// schema.go
// EnsureCollection creates the collection with validator {$jsonSchema: jsonSchema}, validationLevel "strict"
// and validationAction "error", or, if the collection exists, applies that definition with collMod. Idempotent.
func EnsureCollection(ctx context.Context, db *mongo.Database, name string, jsonSchema bson.D) error
// EnsureIndexes creates the indexes on collection. Idempotent for identical definitions.
func EnsureIndexes(ctx context.Context, db *mongo.Database, collection string, models ...mongo.IndexModel) error

// testdb_test.go
func newTestDB(t *testing.T) *mongo.Database
```

Lock semantics. This is the part that must be right:
- tryAcquire is a single atomic UpdateOne with upsert on {_id: "schema"}. Its filter also requires (owner == ours OR expiresAt <= now), and it sets owner and expiresAt = now + lease.
- A duplicate-key error (mongo.IsDuplicateKeyError) from that upsert means another live instance holds the lock: return (false, nil). Return any other error.
- Prefer the server clock ($$NOW via $expr and an aggregation-pipeline update), so instances with skewed clocks agree on expiry. If you use the client clock instead, explain why in a comment.
- renew and release filter on {_id: "schema", owner: ours}. renew returns ErrSchemaLockLost when MatchedCount is 0.
- An expired lock left by a dead instance is taken over by the next tryAcquire. An unexpired lock of another owner is never taken.

Ledger rules: a migration is recorded only after its Up step succeeded; the caller ensures this. `_id` is the migration ID, so recording the same migration twice fails.

Helper rules:
- EnsureCollection must tolerate an existing collection, including one created concurrently: a NamespaceExists error (code 48) falls through to collMod.
- Running EnsureCollection again with the same definition changes nothing: the collection's listCollections options are identical before and after. With a changed jsonSchema, the new definition takes effect.
- Build schemas with bson.D, never bson.M, so definitions are deterministic.
- EnsureIndexes requires an explicit name on every IndexModel and returns an error otherwise. Identical definitions are a no-op.
- EnsureIndexes doesn't support changing an existing index's definition. Return MongoDB's conflict error wrapped with the index name, and say in the doc comment that the fix is a new migration that drops the index first.

Tests use `package mongodb` (internal tests). They are integration tests that run only when MONGO_TEST_URI is set and call t.Skip otherwise. newTestDB lives in testdb_test.go, and migrate_test.go will reuse it later. It:
- connects with the existing Connect;
- uses its own database `test_<random hex>` (crypto/rand), because parallel agents share the server;
- drops that database and disconnects in t.Cleanup.

Cover:
- Ledger:
  - an empty ledger returns no IDs;
  - after recording, appliedMigrationIDs returns the IDs sorted, and the fields are stored as appliedAt (a date) and durationMs (int64);
  - recording the same ID twice fails.
- Lock:
  - a free lock is acquired;
  - a second owner can't acquire it while it's held;
  - the same owner can re-acquire it;
  - renew extends expiresAt;
  - renew returns ErrSchemaLockLost after another owner took over an expired lease;
  - release lets another owner acquire;
  - release by a non-owner deletes nothing;
  - a lock document inserted directly with expiresAt in the past (a dead instance) is taken over;
  - acquire waits and succeeds once a short lease (e.g. 1 s) expires;
  - acquire returns when ctx is cancelled;
  - when N goroutines race tryAcquire on a fresh database, exactly one wins.
- Helpers:
  - EnsureCollection creates the collection with the validator and levels strict/error, and rejects an invalid document (WriteException code 121);
  - running it again leaves the listCollections options unchanged;
  - running it with a changed schema applies the new one: a document that was valid before is now rejected, or vice versa;
  - EnsureIndexes run twice yields the same index list;
  - an IndexModel without a name is an error.
Keep the tests fast with short leases and polls. If MONGO_TEST_URI is set in your environment, these tests must pass.

Coordination: only create ledger.go, lock.go, schema.go, their _test.go files and testdb_test.go. Give extra unexported helpers specific names, also in test files (e.g. lockFilter, ledgerTestEntry), so nothing collides with migration.go and migration_test.go. backend/check.sh must pass.
